\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\input{macros}
\title{Load Test Report: Glulam Beam Prototypes}
\author{Structural Lab, Group B}
\date{April 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\newcommand{\avg}[1]{\overline{#1}}
\section{Setup}
Three glulam beam prototypes were fabricated from spruce lamellae.
Each beam spanned 3.6 m between simple supports.
Load was applied at midspan with a hydraulic actuator.
The loading rate followed the lab standard protocol.
Deflection was recorded with two displacement transducers.
Strain gauges were bonded near the tension face.
The mean failure load is denoted $\avg{P}$ in what follows.
The mean midspan deflection is denoted $\avg{\delta}$.
All specimens were conditioned at 65 percent relative humidity.
Moisture content was checked before each test.
\section{Results}
All three beams failed in bending at the tension face.
The mean failure load $\avg{P}$ was 41.2 kN.
Individual values ranged from 39.5 kN to 43.0 kN.
The mean midspan deflection $\avg{\delta}$ at failure was 28 mm.
Failure initiated at finger joints in two of the three beams.
The third beam failed along a knot cluster.
Load-deflection curves remained linear up to about 70 percent of $\avg{P}$.
No shear cracks were observed before peak load.
\section{Discussion}
The scatter in failure load is consistent with earlier batches.
Knot clusters and finger joints remain the governing defects.
The measured stiffness matches the design value within 5 percent.
A larger sample is planned for the autumn campaign.
\end{document}

